{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "05", "section": "02", "chapter_title": "AI-Enhanced Presentation Development", "title": "Slide Structure Before Decoration", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting slide structure before decoration to presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development", "cat100_supplement_slide_structure_before_decoration", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 2, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/05/01"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_5_2_slide_structure_before_decoration_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_5_2_slide_structure_before_decoration_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_5_2_slide_structure_before_decoration_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_5_2_slide_structure_before_decoration_calibration"]}, "content": "# Slide Structure Before Decoration\n\n![Slide Structure Before Decoration textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/05-02-slide-structure-before-decoration.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nWhen a slide is hard to follow, the usual instinct is to add: a brighter theme, an icon, an animation. That instinct treats a structure problem as a decoration problem, and it almost always makes the slide worse. A slide whose title is the word \"Results\" cannot be rescued by a good color palette, because the reader still does not know what the result was.\n\nThis section teaches you to build the message first and the look last. The core technique is the assertion headline (a complete claim in the title bar) supported by a small number of evidence points, with theme and styling applied only after the message survives on its own. The artifact, your presentation storyboard, is where you can show a graders or a teammate that the structure carries the point before any styling exists.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA team rehearses a deck and keeps explaining each slide out loud: \"So this one is about response times, and basically the point is it got faster.\" If you have to narrate the point of a slide, the slide is not carrying the point; you are. The fix is not a transition or a stock photo. The fix is to put the spoken sentence (\"Response time dropped from 4.2s to 1.1s after caching\") into the title bar, where it belongs, and let the bullets become the proof.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA slide has two jobs: state one idea and prove it. The structure that does both is the **assertion-evidence** pattern. The title is a full sentence that asserts the slide's single claim. The body holds the minimum evidence that supports that claim, typically two or three items, not six. Everything else (theme, font, icon, animation) is decoration, which is legitimate only after the assertion and evidence already work in plain text.\n\nTwo failure modes are worth naming because they look productive but are not:\n\n- **Label titles.** A title like \"Results,\" \"Overview,\" or \"Data\" names a topic instead of making a claim. It forces the reader to hunt the bullets for the point, and it lets vague filler bullets survive because nothing has to support a real assertion.\n- **Decoration-first revision.** Choosing a theme, then trying to fit a message into it, inverts the dependency. The theme should serve the message; when it leads, you end up protecting a pretty layout instead of a clear point.\n\nThis is the data-ink idea applied to slides: every visual element should carry meaning. An assertion headline carries the claim; the supporting points carry the proof; a decorative element that carries neither is ink the reader has to wade through. The structural test is brutal and useful: strip all styling, leave black text on white, and ask whether the slide still makes its point. If it does not, no theme will save it.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nGoal: fix one slide whose message is buried, before any theme is applied.\n\nWeak slide (decoration-first):\n\n```\nTitle: Results\n- We did a survey\n- There were many responses\n- The tool was used by the team\n- It saved some time\n- People liked it\n- More details in the report\n```\n\nStructured rewrite (message-first). Replace the label title with an assertion headline and keep at most three supporting points:\n\n```\nTitle: Response time dropped from 4.2s to 1.1s after caching\n- 4,000 requests measured before and after\n- Same hardware, same test script\n- Recommendation: ship caching to production\n```\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Read the old title \"Results\"; it states no point, so rewrite it as a claim with a number.\n2. Cut the six vague bullets to three that support that one claim.\n3. Only now consider a theme.\n\nNotice what the structure forced out. \"People liked it\" had no number and supported no claim, so it could not survive once the title became a real assertion. The headline now carries the message, and a reader gets the point even if the projector renders the slide in plain black and white.\n\nWork the rewrite one decision at a time, predicting each move before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Rebuild the buried slide, message first\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The slide title is the single word \\\"Results.\\\" What is the first thing to do?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Pick a cleaner theme\",\"Rewrite the title as a complete claim with a number\",\"Add an icon to the title bar\",\"Animate the bullets in one at a time\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Rewrite the label into an assertion: \\\"Response time dropped from 4.2s to 1.1s after caching.\\\" The title now states a claim a reader can agree or disagree with.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You now have six vague bullets. What should happen to them?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Keep all six so the slide looks thorough\",\"Cut to at most three that directly support the new claim\",\"Bold the most important one\",\"Move them into the speaker notes\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Cut to three supporting points: 4,000 requests measured, same hardware and script, recommendation to ship. \\\"People liked it\\\" supports no claim and cannot survive.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Only now consider a theme. Because the message already works in plain black on white, any styling is the last coat, not the rescue.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teammate says the slide is weak and suggests applying a bolder theme to fix it. Why is that the wrong first move?\" explanation=\"A theme is decoration, the last layer. The slide is weak because its title is a label, not a claim. Styling cannot make a reader understand a point the structure never stated.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The theme is the right fix; bolder colors clarify the point\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The structure problem (a label title) must be fixed before any styling\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The slide just needs more bullets\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Animation would carry the missing meaning\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBuild the message before the look: write the title as a complete claim, attach only the evidence that supports it, and apply styling last. If stripping the theme breaks the slide, the slide was never structured; it was decorated.\n\nUse this check when a slide looks finished but may still be hiding its point:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Read only the title. Does it state a claim a reader could agree or disagree with?\n2. **Evidence check:** Does each remaining point support that exact claim, and could any be cut without losing the proof?\n3. **Revision check:** What label title did you turn into an assertion, and which bullets did you drop and why?\n\nIf the title is a topic word rather than a claim, fix the structure before you touch the design.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nOne misreading is thinking the assertion-evidence rule means \"fewer words always wins.\" The goal is not minimal text; it is a title that makes a claim and evidence that proves it. A three-word title that says nothing is worse than a longer title that states the point.\n\nA second misreading is accepting an AI or template suggestion because the result looks more polished, without checking whether it preserved the claim. A redesign that centers a beautiful image but demotes the headline to a caption has traded the message for the look.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the pattern across a sequence, not just one slide. Take a five-slide deck and read only the titles in order, ignoring everything else. If the titles by themselves tell a coherent story (claim, claim, claim), the deck is structured. If the titles read \"Intro, Background, Data, Results, Conclusion,\" you have a labeled outline, not an argument, and a busy reader skimming the title bar learns nothing.\n\nNow rewrite the title row alone: \"Our reports took two days, so analysts fell behind\" leading to \"Caching cut response time to 1.1s\" leading to \"We recommend shipping caching this sprint.\" You have not touched a single theme or image, yet the deck now makes its case from the titles down. That is the order the work belongs in: structure that stands on its own first, decoration on top of it second.\n\nOrder the assertion-evidence build so each step depends on the one before it:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Write the title as a complete claim a reader could agree or disagree with\", \"Attach only the evidence that directly supports that claim\", \"Cut every bullet that supports no claim\", \"Strip all styling and confirm the slide still makes its point\", \"Apply theme and font as the final coat\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: on the left, each slide's title; on the right, whether it is a claim or a label and what evidence supports it. Flag every label title and every bullet that supports nothing. By the end of 05.02, your note should let a reader see the deck's argument from the titles alone.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which titles are claims and which are just topic labels?\n2. For each claim, what is the minimum evidence that proves it?\n3. Which element is decoration that carries no meaning?\n4. Would the slide still make its point with all styling removed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe storyboard is strong when its titles, read alone, carry the argument, and every supporting point earns its place. Treat the theme as the final coat, applied only after the plain-text version already works. If a slide depends on its styling to make sense, mark the gap and rebuild the structure first.\n\nA useful note for the artifact would sound like this: \"I changed slide 3's title from 'Results' to 'Response time dropped from 4.2s to 1.1s after caching' and cut three filler bullets that supported no claim. I accepted the AI's tighter wording of the recommendation, but rejected its suggestion to add an animated build, because the animation carried no meaning the static slide lacked.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same content to a one-page printed handout with no slide software and no animation at all. The assertion-evidence structure transfers directly: the headline becomes the section heading, the supporting points become the body, and there is no theme to hide behind. If your slide collapses into a clear handout without losing anything, the structure was real. If it falls apart without its visual styling, the decoration was doing work the structure should have done.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect the ideas this section builds on. See how an assertion headline feeds evidence-based revision of the structure:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_slide_structure_before_decoration\", \"cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/05-02-slide-structure-before-decoration-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"05.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation of assertion-evidence titles tied to data-ink reasoning.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a label-title slide with its assertion-headline rewrite.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the headline-first rule to a slide in a different deck.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where rewriting a title forced a weak bullet to be cut.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence for CAT 100 05.02. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_slide_structure_before_decoration\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm every slide title states a claim, every supporting point proves that claim, and your trace records at least one label title you turned into an assertion and one decorative element you rejected.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_5_2_slide_structure_before_decoration_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_slide_structure_before_decoration", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_slide_structure_before_decoration", "cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development"], "stem": "You have a 5-slide deck. Which version shows structure was built before decoration?", "options": ["Every slide has a gradient background and entrance animations, but the titles read 'Slide 1', 'Slide 2'.", "Each slide has an assertion headline (for example 'Sales rose 18% after the redesign') and at most three supporting points; no theme applied yet.", "One slide holds a 9-bullet paragraph copied from the report.", "The deck uses three different fonts and clip art on every slide."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Assertion headlines plus limited support are the structure; decoration is deferred until the message arc is clear.", "difficulty": "easy", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_5_2_slide_structure_before_decoration_wall_of_bullets"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_5_2_slide_structure_before_decoration_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "Which note about your draft gives BigAL a structural problem to work on?", "options": ["These slides need to pop more.", "Slides 2 and 3 both argue 'the tool saved time', so the headline arc repeats instead of advancing. How do I split them?", "I like the colors.", "Almost done."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The note names a concrete structural fault (a repeated headline claim) so feedback can target the arc.", "difficulty": "medium", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_5_2_slide_structure_before_decoration_decorate_first"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_5_2_slide_structure_before_decoration_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ai_supported_learning", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"], "stem": "How can you use AI on slide structure without handing over your judgment?", "options": ["Ask AI to apply a flashy template and call the deck finished.", "Paste your six slide headlines and ask AI whether the arc has gaps or repeats, then you decide what to reord